WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:05.080
Okay, one more thing.

00:05.080 --> 00:09.280
In order to maintain our indexes is by updating the statistics.

00:09.280 --> 00:15.600
The database engines usually use statistics in order to understand which index should be used for our

00:15.600 --> 00:21.080
query, and if these statistics are not up to date, SQL can make wrong decisions.

00:21.080 --> 00:22.640
So let's understand what this means.

00:22.680 --> 00:27.880
Now let's say that you have created a table and you start inserting data to this new table.

00:27.920 --> 00:32.800
Now the database engine can go and create a new table and insert the data behind the scenes.

00:32.800 --> 00:37.320
The database engine can go and create for your new table statistics.

00:37.320 --> 00:40.240
It's like metadata information about your data.

00:40.440 --> 00:46.720
And that's like a report or insights about your table where you can find a lot of information like the

00:46.720 --> 00:50.640
number of rows, the distribution of values in a column.

00:50.640 --> 00:58.360
And as well we can find the number of distinct values and histograms and patterns and many other informations

00:58.360 --> 00:59.480
about your table.

00:59.480 --> 01:03.810
So now of course the question is why do we have those informations in the database.

01:03.850 --> 01:07.010
Now imagine that you are doing select from where.

01:07.050 --> 01:07.850
What's going to happen.

01:07.850 --> 01:11.530
The database engine has to go and create an execution plan.

01:11.770 --> 01:14.050
We're going to learn about this later in details.

01:14.050 --> 01:17.490
It is just a roadmap on how to execute this query.

01:17.490 --> 01:22.170
So here for example in order to load the data from the table, there are like different ways on how

01:22.170 --> 01:22.970
to do it.

01:22.970 --> 01:26.610
So there is like a table scan index scan index seek.

01:26.770 --> 01:31.850
So that means the database engine has here three different ways on how to do it.

01:31.890 --> 01:38.490
And now in order for the database to decide which way to use, it's going to go and read the statistics

01:38.490 --> 01:39.490
of the table.

01:39.490 --> 01:41.490
So it's going to go and collect informations okay.

01:41.530 --> 01:42.930
How many rows do we have.

01:42.970 --> 01:44.770
Are the informations are unique.

01:44.810 --> 01:47.490
How is the distribution of the data and so on.

01:47.490 --> 01:53.930
And now based on those statistics and numbers the database can now make a good decision about which

01:53.970 --> 01:56.570
methods to use in order to load the data.

01:56.570 --> 02:01.170
So for example here the index scan is the best way to load our table.

02:01.170 --> 02:07.780
So this is exactly why the database needs the statistics in order to make the correct decision and to

02:07.820 --> 02:09.500
use the correct index.

02:09.500 --> 02:13.060
So now you might ask okay, this is something internal for the database.

02:13.260 --> 02:14.780
Why do we have to care about it.

02:14.820 --> 02:16.100
Well there is an issue.

02:16.140 --> 02:18.740
Now for example in our table we have 50 rows.

02:18.780 --> 02:24.260
And let's say that in the next day you went and inserted to this table like around 1 million row.

02:24.460 --> 02:29.940
Now the issue that could happen is that the statistics will not get updated about this table.

02:29.940 --> 02:33.540
And the statistics can still say that we have only 50 rows.

02:33.580 --> 02:37.740
So that means the statistics of this table is now outdated.

02:37.740 --> 02:44.260
And the big issue that once you query this table, the SQL engine does not know at all about the 1 million

02:44.260 --> 02:49.340
row that you have inserted in the table, because it's going to go and ask the statistics, and it's

02:49.340 --> 02:51.380
going to answer with only 50 rows.

02:51.420 --> 02:56.460
And the database is going to say, okay, this is very small table and let's maybe skip an index or

02:56.460 --> 02:57.020
something.

02:57.020 --> 03:03.060
So that means the database can make wrong decisions because the statistics are outdated.

03:03.420 --> 03:08.670
And now your task is to monitor those statistics and to keep updating them.

03:08.670 --> 03:10.310
So let's see how we can do that.

03:10.350 --> 03:10.590
Okay.

03:10.630 --> 03:16.310
So now the first thing that we have to do is to find out whether our statistics are up to date or outdated.

03:16.430 --> 03:20.630
In order to do that, we have as well to access the metadata about our database.

03:20.630 --> 03:26.790
And for that as well, we have tables and dynamic management functions in the system schema, where

03:26.790 --> 03:29.510
we can find a lot of details about the statistics.

03:29.510 --> 03:33.710
And in order to monitor the statistics, I have prepared a query like this.

03:33.710 --> 03:39.550
So here I'm using a table called stats where here you're going to get a list of all statistics inside

03:39.550 --> 03:42.270
our database and the name of the statistics.

03:42.270 --> 03:45.870
And then I'm joining it with the tables in order to get the table name.

03:45.870 --> 03:49.670
And what is very important is that dynamic management function.

03:49.670 --> 03:54.790
So here we're going to get very important informations like the last updates and the number of rows

03:54.830 --> 03:57.150
and the number of modifications.

03:57.150 --> 03:59.150
So let's go and query it.

03:59.150 --> 04:03.350
So here we can see informations like the table name the statistics name.

04:03.350 --> 04:07.750
And now it's very important when the last time the statistics get updated.

04:07.750 --> 04:10.920
So now let's go and check our table DB customers.

04:10.920 --> 04:13.040
We can see here that statistics name.

04:13.040 --> 04:15.880
And what is very important is the last update.

04:15.880 --> 04:18.920
So this tells us how old is the statistics.

04:18.920 --> 04:20.680
So for me it is like four days.

04:20.720 --> 04:23.640
And then we can find the total number of rows in this table.

04:23.680 --> 04:29.480
And now what is very important is the number of modifications that have been done on the table.

04:29.480 --> 04:36.360
So after updating the statistics on the 19 of October, there were around 15 rows that got modification.

04:36.360 --> 04:39.760
And this could be an insert update delete.

04:39.760 --> 04:42.800
So any operation on the table is considered to be a modification.

04:42.800 --> 04:45.760
So with that you can see there were a lot of modifications.

04:45.760 --> 04:47.800
So this statistics should be updated.

04:47.800 --> 04:52.480
So now for the table customers you can see that the statistics are up to date.

04:52.480 --> 04:55.000
So we have here zero as a modifications.

04:55.000 --> 04:57.840
And there will be no need to update the statistics.

04:57.840 --> 05:03.160
So this is how you can go and check the statistics informations inside your database in order to make

05:03.160 --> 05:03.920
a decision.

05:03.920 --> 05:06.280
Should I update the statistics or not.

05:06.320 --> 05:10.880
So now let's say that I would like to go and update the statistics of our table DB customers.

05:11.240 --> 05:14.000
Now as you can see we have here multiple statistics.

05:14.160 --> 05:16.600
So over here we have these statistics on this table.

05:16.600 --> 05:19.640
And as well we have the statistics on the index.

05:19.760 --> 05:25.760
So as you can see we have here multiple statistics in one table one for the table itself and one for

05:25.760 --> 05:27.760
each index that we have in this table.

05:27.800 --> 05:31.880
So now let's say that I would like to go and update the statistics only for one.

05:31.920 --> 05:35.760
I don't want to update everything in this table only for one statistics.

05:35.760 --> 05:37.280
Let's go and do that.

05:37.520 --> 05:40.520
So it's going to be very simple update statistics.

05:41.080 --> 05:42.720
And then you have to go and mention the name.

05:42.720 --> 05:45.080
So it's going to be sales DB customers.

05:45.600 --> 05:48.280
And then we have to specify the name of that statistic.

05:48.280 --> 05:52.600
So let's go and get this over here and let's go and execute it.

05:53.280 --> 05:54.720
So it was very fast.

05:54.720 --> 05:58.680
Let's go and re execute our query and check the data.

05:58.720 --> 06:00.320
So now let's go and find it.

06:00.360 --> 06:02.240
It was exactly this one.

06:02.240 --> 06:04.560
And as you can see it just got updated.

06:04.560 --> 06:08.800
And the number of rows is five and the number of modifications is zero.

06:08.800 --> 06:12.760
So we have now an up to date statistics for this table.

06:12.800 --> 06:17.490
But let's say that I would like to go and update the rest, but I don't want to do it one by one.

06:17.650 --> 06:24.130
So what we can do, we can just copy the same thing over here, but we don't specify any name of the

06:24.130 --> 06:24.850
statistic.

06:25.090 --> 06:28.570
So we are saying update statistics and then only the table name.

06:28.690 --> 06:30.090
So let's go and execute it.

06:30.090 --> 06:34.850
So now what's going to happen is we're going to go and update all the statistics that belongs to this

06:34.850 --> 06:35.370
table.

06:35.530 --> 06:37.770
So let's go and check our query again.

06:37.890 --> 06:39.850
Now you can see everything disappeared.

06:39.850 --> 06:45.610
And the DB customer is completely up to date with no modifications problem.

06:45.690 --> 06:48.170
So this is how you can go and update your table.

06:48.170 --> 06:50.730
And you can do then for the rest as well.

06:50.730 --> 06:56.810
But now there is like one more thing where you can go and update the statistics of the whole database.

06:57.130 --> 07:03.210
But beware, this might take really long time, and we're going to do that by executing a special stored

07:03.210 --> 07:03.850
procedure.

07:03.930 --> 07:07.570
So execute ESP update stats.

07:07.930 --> 07:09.050
This one over here.

07:09.090 --> 07:11.490
Let's go and do that.

07:11.530 --> 07:12.530
And now it is done.

07:12.530 --> 07:14.690
And we have here a pretty long log.

07:14.930 --> 07:17.740
It was fast because we don't have a big database.

07:17.740 --> 07:22.140
It is a very small database, so it's not compared to any real databases.

07:22.140 --> 07:27.300
So now you can see over here that SQL is going through everything that you have in the database and

07:27.300 --> 07:29.020
trying to update the statistics.

07:29.020 --> 07:33.340
So in many situations it's going to be not necessary because there is nothing to update.

07:33.340 --> 07:35.740
There were no modifications and so on.

07:35.780 --> 07:40.420
That's why the database is smart enough to say no, it is not required and it go and skip it.

07:40.420 --> 07:46.260
So now how I usually do it in my project that I have like a job in the weekend where it's going to go

07:46.260 --> 07:49.380
and update the whole database statistics.

07:49.380 --> 07:55.260
So with that, I make sure all my tables and indexes having up to date statistics.

07:55.300 --> 07:58.940
Of course, if you have small database, you can run this like every day.

07:59.100 --> 08:02.540
But if this takes a long time then you can schedule it in the weekends.

08:02.580 --> 08:08.340
And as well, if I know in the project that there will be in one day a lot of new incoming data.

08:08.540 --> 08:10.820
So we are doing some kind of data migrations.

08:10.820 --> 08:15.980
So I go and update the statistics after the data migration is done, just to make sure we have up to

08:16.020 --> 08:17.060
date statistics.

08:17.060 --> 08:20.820
So this is how we monitor and update the statistics of the database.
